---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav('questions-to-ask-before-you-hire-a-digital-agency')
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        Picking an agency mostly comes down to trust before you have any real proof. The questions below will not
        remove all the risk, but they will surface a lot of it before you have signed anything or spent a dollar.
      </p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>What would you actually do if you have not solved this exact problem before?</strong> Every agency
            runs into things they have not built before, that is normal. What matters is whether they have a real process
            for figuring it out, discovery, prototyping, technical testing, or whether they just reach for whatever they
            already know regardless of fit.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>What technology are you actually using, and does it match what you are being quoted for?</strong> We
            once had a client come to us with a quote from another agency for a native iOS and Android app. When we looked
            into it, the technology behind the quote was actually a cross-platform framework, not native. The client was
            being charged native app pricing for a lower-tier build. Ask directly, and ask them to explain the tradeoffs
            of their recommendation in plain language, not just the name of the technology. If you want a deeper read on
            this, we cover it in
            <a
              href="/insights/how-to-evaluate-the-technology-your-agency-recommends"
              class="underline underline-offset-2">how to evaluate the technology your agency recommends</a
            >.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Who will actually be doing the work?</strong> Senior staff in the pitch meeting, then a junior or outsourced
            team on delivery, is a common pattern. Ask who is actually assigned before you sign anything.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Where is the delivery team actually based?</strong> This affects response times, working-hour overlap,
            potential language barriers, and whether the team is familiar with local technical or regulatory requirements,
            things like Australian accessibility standards or local payment providers. Off-shoring is not automatically a
            problem, we have written about
            <a href="/insights/what-about-off-shoring" class="underline underline-offset-2">when it works</a>, but it is
            something you should know upfront rather than discover later.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Who owns the IP?</strong> The code, the designs, the content, all of it. Some agencies build in a way
            that quietly keeps you dependent on them rather than in full ownership of what you paid for.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Is there anything in the solution that comes with a recurring fee, and who does it go to?</strong> A
            hosting fee is normal. A recurring fee that goes straight to the agency for something you could otherwise own
            outright is worth asking more about. It might be entirely justified. It might also be a way of keeping you
            locked in.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Can they show you references from something a similar size and complexity to your project?</strong> A
            portfolio full of small brochure sites tells you very little about how an agency handles a complex build.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-1 lg:mt-1.5" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>How fast did the quote come, and how many real questions came before it?</strong> A number that arrives
            before anyone has asked about your actual requirements is not a considered estimate.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        None of these questions guarantee a good outcome on their own, but an agency that answers them clearly and
        without getting defensive is telling you something useful. If you would rather have a second opinion on a
        quote or proposal before you commit to it, that is exactly the kind of thing a consulting engagement can help
        with.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk />
</Layout>
